n-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Kalhan</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Barriers to adherence to chronic obstructive pulmonary disease guidelines by primary care physicians</article-title><source>Int J Chron Obstruct Pulm Dis</source><year>2011</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>171</fpage><lpage>179</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="b21-copd-6-659"><label>21</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cabana</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Rand</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Powe</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Why don&#x02019;t physicians follow clinical practice guidelines? A framework for improvement</article-title><source>JAMA</source><year>1999</year><volume>282</volume><issue>15</issue><fpage>1458</fpage><lpage>1465</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10535437</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b22-copd-6-659"><label>22</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Carlsen</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Norheim</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>&#x0201c;What lies beneath it all?&#x0201d; &#x02013; an interview study of GP&#x02019;s attitudes to the use of guidelines</article-title><source>BMC Health Serv Res</source><year>2008</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>218</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18945360</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b23-copd-6-659"><label>23</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sarc</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Jeric</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Ziherl</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Adherence to treatment guidelines and long-term survival in hospitalized patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease</article-title><source>J Eval Clin Pract</source><year>2011</year><volume>17</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>737</fpage><lpage>743</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">21223458</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b24-copd-6-659"><label>24</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Serginson</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Armstrong</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Variability in the rate of prescription and cost of domiciliary oxygen therapy in Australia</article-title><source>Med J Aust</source><year>2009</year><volume>191</volume><issue>10</issue><fpage>549</fpage><lpage>553</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19912087</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b25-copd-6-659"><label>25</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Guyatt</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>McKim</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Austin</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Appropriateness of domiciliary oxygen delivery</article-title><source>Chest</source><year>2000</year><volume>118</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>1303</fpage><lpage>1308</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11083678</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b26-copd-6-659"><label>26</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Partridge</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Getting the rehabilitation message across: emerging barriers and positive health benefits</article-title><source>Eur Respir J</source><year>2009</year><volume>34</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>2</fpage><lpage>4</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">19567595</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="b27-copd-6-659"><label>27</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Keating</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Holland</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>What prevents people with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease from attending pulmonary rehabilitation?</article-title></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="f1-copd-6-659" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Relationship between complexity of behavior change and ease of implementation for COPD care recommendations.</p><p><bold>Abbreviation:</bold> COPD, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="copd-6-659f1"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-copd-6-659" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Categories derived from data analysis for the six COPD recommendations</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Smoking cessation</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Long term oxygen</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Influenza vaccination</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Guideline based medications</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pulmonary rehabilitation (PR)</th><th align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Plan and advice for future exacerbations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Major categories<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn1-copd-6-659">*</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Minor categories<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn2-copd-6-659">#</xref></td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Role clarity</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Identified as role (in GP &#x0003e; hospital)<break/>Efficacy of role (in GP &#x0003e; hospital setting)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Delegation (to respiratory specialist)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Identified as role (GP and hospital)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Identified as role (GP and hospital)</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Infrequently identified as part of regular role</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Identified as role (GP and hospital) but nature of involvement varied</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Persuasive communication</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Use of persistence<break/>Non blaming of patient<break/>Tailoring advice<break/>Make use of opportunities<break/>Frequently addressed<break/>Perceived health benefit</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Frequently addressed<break/>Perceived health benefit</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Infrequently addressed</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Variable belief in health benefit<break/>Variable instructions to patients</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Complexity of behavior change</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Well established process<break/>Clear eligibility criteria</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">High level organization required of patient (cognitive ability, time, money)<break/>Difficult to find referral information<break/>Access difficult for patients</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Highly complex set of behaviors required by patient (decision-making, self-management, symptom recognition)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Awareness and support</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Established organizational support<break/>Established clinician resources<break/>Multiple treatment options<break/>Supported by other health professionals</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Clinician awareness of health benefit</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">High public and clinician awareness<break/>Established organizational support</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Established organizational support (hospital pharmacists hospital and GP practice nurses)<break/>High clinician awareness and support</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Low awareness of eligibility and referral process</td><td align="left" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lack of follow-up community based support</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1-copd-6-659"><label>*</label><p><bold>Notes:</bold> Major categories derived from data analysis were (1) role clarity of the medical practitioner; (2) persuasive communication with the patient; (3) complexity of behavioral change required of the patient and medical practitioner; (4) awareness and support available at multiple levels;</p></fn><fn id="tfn2-copd-6-659"><label>#</label><p>minor categories reflect subunits of data contributing to the major category and consist of barriers to, or enablers of, recommendation implementation.</p></fn><fn id="tfn3-copd-6-659"><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> COPD, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; GP, general practitioner.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>